A||||9||Coconut||Has anyone here bought one of these?||||5||
Z||000000||Coconut||10-10-2000||02:00 AM||||It's time to replace all four brake hoses on my ride and was pricing brake hoses online. Fiero store offers their regular, plain brake hoses for roughly 26.00 a piece but I also noticed that they have a product called Earl's braided steel brake hoses for about 90.00 for a set of 4!  It has a teflon inner lining with stainless steel braiding ( now, that's gotta look pretty sharp! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).<P>My question: Are these brake hoses actually any better than regular rubber hoses, or are they just eye candy with no practical advantage?  Has anyone here ever bought these? If you have, are you happy with your purchase?, was it a fairly straight forward installation or were there any special tools or hardware involved?   Any comments would be appreciated!<P>Thanks guys, <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||10-10-2000||02:09 AM||||i think the braided steel is supposed to not expand when you hit the brakes like a regular rubber hose would,so all pedal pressure is applied to the piston for stronger stopping action. i need to replace mine too but rubber is good enough for me. auto zone $14 apiece.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||10-10-2000||02:18 AM||||Yes teflon lined stanless is good. They are aircraft quality and shouldnt need replacing for as long as you own the care. I have heard they give a firmer pedal. I will upgrade to these when I need to replace the stock ones.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||10-10-2000||03:11 AM||||Bought 'em and installed 'em... well, installed half of them anyways.  I couldn't get the bleeder screws on the driver's side front/rear to budge, so I haven't replaced those hoses yet.  Installation is simple, assuming none of your brake hardware is frozen solid.. <P>Oh yeah, they look really cool too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">       <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||10-10-2000||08:42 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberauto.com</A>  thats where i got my braided lines from.. their goodrich. they seem nice...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||10-10-2000||08:59 AM||||I bought the steel braided brake lines from the Fiero Store. Although the AD says "Earls" they are made by goodrich. I've had them for 2 years now and the pedal is better. The pedal is firm and slightly more sensitive. As soon as the pedal is depressed you can feel the brakes start to work.<BR>I would highly recommend them.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||Rodney||10-10-2000||09:05 AM||||I was interested in the braded steel lines for my 88 Mera. I called a few of the larger circle track shops and asked what they thought of the steel braded lines. They all told me the same thing. They will not make any difference in the feel of the pedal. They are only for preventing damage to the line and are much less likely to be cut during a race. Most people feel a beter pedal as they are replacing old worn out lines. If you install brand new rubber lines the take them off and install the braded lines there should be no difference in pedal feel. The teflon lining may have a longer life so that is a benefit over rubber lines.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||10-10-2000||06:43 PM||||Bought 'em.  Still sitting on the floor.  As soon as I get time...||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-10-2000||08:29 PM||||If you figure how long the lines you have have been on there...<P>I've used both. No dif in feel between new parts. Go with whatever you like, the car won't care.<P>Make sure braided metal lines don't rub or bend to tight. you'll chew up the brade if they rub and try will pinch off if bent to tight. (they are harder to slash cut but they wear quick if they constantly rub.) You might want to put a chunk of fuel line or somehting on the spot where you hold them in the support clamp. It can prevent them crimping if they get pulled tight.<P>Rubber line is not prone to tight bends. It tends to fight them. Rubber lines usually have wear guards on them in places they are prone to rub. Depends on who made them.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Coconut||10-10-2000||09:10 PM||||Thanks to everyone for their input. Looks like I'll be purchasing these hoses! <P><BR>Cruisin' On, <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||14
